{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target falsely presents a dilemma by claiming the preference for corroborated theories is defensible 'only if' we redefine probability to mean corroboration. This ignores Popper's actual solution: that preferring better-corroborated theories is a rational decision based on critical discussion and the aim of truth (verisimilitude), not an inductive calculation of probability. The target fails to make the 'strongest case' for the defense (that no redefinition is needed) as required by the problem context, instead constructing a strawman that forces a concession Popper never made.","premise":"Popper explicitly argued that acting on the best-tested theory is a rational choice without implying it is more probable, distinguishing between the logic of justification (induction/probability) and the pragmatics of action/critical preference.","successor_question":"How can the target reconcile its claim that redefinition is the 'only' defense with Popper's own writings where he defends the preference for corroborated theories without adopting any form of probabilistic confirmation?","target":"f3d844769f6a642144498ae246a7113529c8b3452ba77763a83abac7ae2ed13f"}]}